@@ -0,0 +1,325 @@
+# Conservative coastal pruning (API 1)
+
+This is an **explicit opt-in**, single-forecast isochrone-beam feature. It adds
+sound rejection bounds to an approximate beam, not global optimality. Off is
+unchanged. Lattice and ensemble requests with the option enabled are rejected;
+no solver switch or heuristic fallback occurs.
+
+## Public contract
+
+Include `sailroute/coastal_pruning.hpp` and `sailroute/router.hpp`.
+
+* `coastal_pruning_api_version == 1`;
+  `supports_coastal_pruning(RoutingSolver)` accepts only `isochrone_beam`.
+* `CoastalPruningMode`: `off=0`, `conservative=1`.
+* `CoastalPolygonEdges`: `great_circle=0`,
+  `linear_latitude_longitude=1`.
+* `CoastalBoundStatus`: `available=0`, `unavailable=1`, `disabled=2`.
+* `CoastalTopologyOptions`: `source_identity`, `domain_identity`,
+  `domain` (`EnvironmentCoverage`, static geographic bounds),
+  `polygon_edges`, `clearance_nautical_miles`,
+  `numerical_margin_nautical_miles` (default/minimum 0.0001 nm),
+  `maximum_work` (default 1,000,000 units), `maximum_caps` (1024),
+  `maximum_bytes` (4 MiB), `cancelled`.
+* `CoastalTopology::from_polygons(vector<ExclusionPolygon>,
+  span<const Coordinate> probes, CoastalTopologyOptions)` and
+  `CoastalTopology::from_landmask(const SignedDistanceLandmask&,
+  span<const Coordinate> probes, CoastalTopologyOptions)` return
+  `Result<shared_ptr<const CoastalTopology>>`.
+  Probes are proposals, not asserted land: each is independently certified.
+  Empty polygons/probes give an empty, still valid relaxation.
+* `CoastalTopology::from_certified_caps(vector<CoastalTopology::Cap>,
+  CoastalTopologyOptions)` accepts a caller-certified forbidden subset.
+  `Cap` has `Coordinate center` and `double radius_nautical_miles`.
+  No polygon conversion or secondary enforcement source is required.
+  `caller_certified_caps()` identifies this representation; `native_landmask()`
+  is false and the existing application callback remains final authority.
+* Handle queries: `options()`, `construction_work()`, `cap_count()`,
+  `construction_limited()`, `native_landmask()`, `provider()`,
+  `matches_landmask(mask)`, and
+  `optimistic_distance_nautical_miles(from, destination, arrival_radius,
+  transition_relaxation_nautical_miles=0)`.
+* `SignedDistanceLandmask::shares_data_with(other)` tests exact shared immutable
+  data identity (not merely equal provider strings).
+* `RoutingOptions::coastal_pruning` is a `CoastalPruningOptions` with `mode`,
+  `topology`, `source_identity`, `domain_identity`,
+  `clearance_nautical_miles`, `seed_actions` (`vector<SailingAction>`),
+  `maximum_seed_transitions` (512; zero disables the seed),
+  and optional thread-safe `cancelled` probe.
+* `Router::coastal_remaining_bound(request, position)` returns
+  `Result<CoastalRemainingBound>` with `optimistic_seconds`, `status`,
+  `reason`, and optional `speed_upper_knots`. It validates source/solver
+  compatibility. Unknown speed yields zero and **unavailable**, not infinity.
+* `RouteDiagnostics::coastal_pruning` is optional
+  `CoastalPruningDiagnostics`; absent in Off runs.
+* `RoutingProgressView::post_proof_retained_front` and its owning equivalent
+  explicitly distinguish the new retained population.
+* `RouteResult::coastal_seed_actions` preserves the seed control proposal.
+  `seed_status` says whether the proposal completed validation. Replay uses
+  `evaluate_actions` with the same inputs and pruning Off; do not infer headings
+  from displayed route vertices. Arrival terminates the proposal early.
+
+The C++ additions require rebuilding consumers. They do not authorize growing
+an application's frozen C ABI structures in place.
+
+### Identity, topology, and ownership
+
+The polygon caller certifies that the declared permanently forbidden geometry
+is a subset of the selected enforcement contract. Keep the application callback
+as final authority. Extra application restrictions and timed exclusions can be
+omitted from the proof, **never** from seed/transition validation. A Boolean
+callback alone cannot establish topology. The request's source/domain identity
+and clearance must match the handle; keys must change with geometry revision,
+edge semantics, source uncertainty, domain, or clearance.
+
+Great-circle polygons use the same validated, hemisphere-contained rings and
+holes as exclusions. Multipolygons are accepted. Ambiguous probe containment
+does not produce a cap. Linear latitude/longitude polygons use shortest wrapped
+longitude interpolation and additional certified erosion described below.
+Linear-mode callbacks must reject forbidden **endpoints at every physical
+integration step**; this is satisfied by ordinary polygon segment-intersection
+enforcement. Callbacks that inspect only some steps, ignore land endpoints, or
+use a different projection need a separately certified subset: do not relabel
+them as supported.
+
+Exclusion validation and polygon proof preparation share ring normalization:
+consecutive angular duplicates and repeated closing vertices are removed before
+computing the centroid, winding, or boundary distances. The hemisphere guarantee
+applies to that normalized centroid, never to a duplicate-weighted input ring.
+
+### Caller-certified caps for sampled polygon enforcement
+
+`from_certified_caps` is the preferred adapter when the application can prove
+blocked regions directly with its own polygon library. For example, it can
+prove an axis-aligned wrapped-lonlat rectangle is wholly covered by selected
+land (respecting holes), derive a genuinely inscribed spherical disk, then
+shrink it to account for the enforcement callback's sampling uncertainty.
+The upstream factory does **not** certify that the application's geometric
+proof is true; the caller must certify each submitted open or closed cap is
+permanently forbidden by the actual selected enforcement contract.
+
+If the callback includes segment endpoints and checks samples on the accepted
+spherical connector with a guaranteed maximum along-arc gap of one nautical
+mile, eroding the inscribed disk by at least one nautical mile plus numerical
+uncertainty is sufficient: any connector entering the eroded disk has a sample
+within one nautical mile, inside the original forbidden disk. This requires
+a genuine sampling-gap bound; a nominal spacing setting without that guarantee
+is not a certificate. A Boolean callback or polygon bounding-box intersection
+alone is insufficient. Do not certify rectangles merely because their corners
+are on land, and do not fill holes or unknown coverage.
+
+Native checks canonical finite centers, finite radii strictly between zero and
+a hemisphere (`pi*R/2` nm), nonempty source/domain identity, static domain,
+cancellation, and cap/work/retained-byte budgets. It subtracts the configured
+numerical margin **additionally** from every radius, so open-cap certificates
+are sufficient; caps reduced to zero contribute nothing. Stored caps must lie
+inside the declared preparation coverage. Oversized arrays fail explicitly
+with `resource_limit`, rather than silently dropping input. Each submitted cap
+costs one topology work unit. An empty array is a valid empty relaxation.
+
+Already certified caps do **not** receive polygon-edge homotopy erosion or
+additional full-integration-step erosion, regardless of `options.polygon_edges`:
+the caller has certified the actual enforcement contract already.
+Audit `polygonEdgeModel` is `"caller_certified_caps"`. The existing source/domain
+identity and clearance checks still apply, including every seed transition.
+Outside supplied caps remains relaxed water; there are no disconnection claims.
+
+Native SDF/GSHHG handles share the exact immutable enforcing mask. All signed
+distance errors must be genuine bounds as required by the landmask API.
+Uncertified conservative occupancy raster values are not signed-distance
+certificates and cannot be used as such. Native caps are clipped to both source
+grid coverage and declared preparation coverage. Missing samples add no caps.
+Clearance is relaxed away geometrically but remains part of identity and exact
+enforcement.
+
+The handle is immutable and thread-safe after preparation; it owns caps, keys,
+and a shared reference to native source data when applicable. It has no
+destination-specific cache, forecast state, incumbent, or callback. Preparation
+callbacks are discarded. Keep the handle alive through a request, and share it
+across independent destinations/models only with matching identities.
+
+Work is explicitly bounded in conservative accounting units: polygon validation
+charges 64 per outer vertex and `64 + 8*outer_vertex_count` per hole vertex;
+each polygon probe charges `32*total_vertex_count + 1`; SDF probes charge one.
+The byte setting limits retained logical storage (including reserved caps and
+identity text) and separately bounds estimated polygon preparation storage.
+It is not a process-RSS limit and excludes already supplied source data,
+allocator overhead, and caller allocations. Work-budget exhaustion is a
+`resource_limit` error; cap/retained-byte truncation returns a safe incomplete
+relaxation with `construction_limited()==true`. Unknown regions stay open.
+Cancellation is checked before preparation and between bounded work items.
+
+## Why the bound is admissible
+
+Let the earth be the same sphere used by routing, radius `R=3440.065 nm`.
+
+1. **Certified land subset.** At a proven interior polygon probe `c`, the
+   minimum distance to every outer/hole boundary, less numerical margin, is a
+   spherical cap lying in forbidden land. Winding classification is required
+   to be unambiguous; holes are never filled. SDF probes use
+   `-sample - declared_interpolation_error - margin`. A nonpositive radius
+   contributes nothing. Incomplete coverage cannot create a barrier.
+2. **Linear polygon relaxation.** For each wrapped linear-lonlat edge,
+   `R*(abs(delta_lat)+abs(wrapped_delta_lon))` in radians bounds its length.
+   The linear edge and its minor spherical counterpart have a homotopy inside
+   the first endpoint's cap of that radius, when it is less than a hemisphere.
+   Eroding by the maximum of these radii avoids every swept boundary; membership
+   cannot change there. Longer/ambiguous edges produce no land caps. This is
+   deliberately loose, especially for sparsely sampled coastlines.
+   Routing additionally erodes linear-mode caps by
+   `V_upper * maximum_integration_step`. Every accepted physical segment has
+   legal endpoints and length at most that amount; triangle inequality then
+   prevents its spherical trajectory entering the eroded cap even if the
+   callback tested a linear-lonlat connector.
+3. **Ambient-space relaxation, not a water-node graph.** A cap of radius `r`
+   around surface point `c` is the sphere's intersection with a Euclidean ball
+   centered at `R*c`, radius `q=2R*sin(r/(2R))`. Every legal spherical trajectory
+   lies outside this ball. Allowing paths anywhere in three-dimensional space
+   can only shorten the optimum.
+4. **Exact single-ball formula.** With endpoint distances `x,y` from the ball
+   center and included angle `theta`, an unobstructed segment has its ordinary
+   chord length. Otherwise the shortest ambient detour has length
+
+       sqrt(x*x-q*q) + sqrt(y*y-q*q)
+         + q*(theta-acos(q/x)-acos(q/y)).
+
+   This is two tangent segments and a boundary arc. If an endpoint is inside
+   the relaxed ball, discard that ball's contribution instead of claiming
+   disconnection.
+5. **Whole arrival region.** A spherical arrival circle is inside a Euclidean
+   ball of the same numerical radius around its center. Use a cap contribution
+   only if that entire arrival ball is disjoint from the obstacle ball.
+   A legal arrival endpoint can then be connected to the center within the
+   arrival ball, adding at most the arrival radius. Subtracting that radius is
+   therefore a lower bound. For overlapping arrival balls, omit the cap.
+6. **Combine by maximum.** Take the maximum, **not the sum**, of all single-ball
+   bounds and `max(0, great_circle_distance - arrival_radius)`, then subtract
+   numerical margin. No grid, corridor restriction, interpolation of graph
+   distances, or finite-node shortest path is treated as a lower bound.
+7. **Time.** Without a current provider, `V_upper` is the polar's global
+   interpolated maximum multiplied by the boat factor. Both supported angle
+   interpolants and wind range policies cannot increase that maximum. Sea-state
+   acceleration is an error in exact physics, not an allowed speed; nonnegative
+   maneuver penalties are ignored. With **any current provider**, this version
+   returns zero/unavailable rather than guessing a favorable-current cap.
+   `h=L/V_upper` is thus optimistic over evolving weather. Overflow/unusable
+   speed is also unavailable, never infinity.
+
+Numerical handling uses at least 0.0001 nm geometric erosion, the existing
+arrival acceptance slack, and a strict two-second comparison tolerance for
+whole-second arrival quantization. Ties survive. No geographic commitment,
+bearing cutoff, detour ratio, or current boat speed is used.
+
+## Seed, search, and failure semantics
+
+The default bounded seed proposes one destination-bearing timed heading. A
+caller may instead supply a timed heading policy (including detours). Exact
+`evaluate_heading_transition` integration validates departure, boat/polar,
+evolving forecast, currents, waves, maneuver state, arrival region, horizon,
+callback, native land, and timed exclusions. A geometric path alone is **never**
+an incumbent. Successful complete validation preserves the feasible result.
+Infeasible seeds or an exhausted seed budget are normal, audited outcomes.
+Malformed actions and encountered provider/resource/cancellation errors remain
+errors. The simple default seed is not a general coastal planner.
+
+Parents are checked before worker expansion/resource admission; children are
+checked after exact eligibility and before strategic probes/beam retention.
+Reject only if `state_time + h > horizon + 2s` or
+`state_time + h > validated_incumbent_arrival + 2s`. No disconnection rejection
+is implemented. Reuse a faster validated seed instead of a later main arrival.
+Otherwise keep the existing arrival ordering. Proof exhaustion returns the
+incumbent if present, or a legal best-known partial with the existing
+forecast/duration distinction. Encountered errors are not converted to results.
+Reducing a beam's surviving population can change later beam choices; this
+does not imply general Off/On route equivalence.
+
+## Audit and progress
+
+All counts are cumulative/nondecreasing within one run:
+
+* `parent_expansions_skipped`: parent states rejected before expansion.
+* `candidates_disconnected`: reserved, always zero in API 1.
+* `candidates_horizon`, `candidates_incumbent`: child states rejected for the
+  stated reason; horizon takes precedence if both apply.
+* `bound_unavailable`: parent/child bound attempts using unavailable speed or
+  numeric fallback. It is not a count of unique geographic positions.
+* `seed_transition_evaluations`: attempted heading integration iterations plus
+  maneuver-drift integration intervals, including unsuccessful attempts.
+  This is separate from all main-search counters.
+* `topology_work`: fixed preparation accounting units, not per-route expansions.
+* Existing `pruned_candidates` remains **ordinary beam pruning only**; existing
+  expanded/generated/retained counts remain main-search work only.
+
+The audit also includes requested/effective mode, source/domain identities,
+domain coordinates and longitude coverage, polygon edge model, topology cap
+count, limited flag, clearance, numerical margin, seed budget/status,
+bound status, optional speed bound, and optional incumbent arrival.
+
+Seed progress has `seed_status="running"` and no main frontier geometry.
+Conservative main progress always constructs `destination_front` from the
+post-proof/post-beam retained nodes, even when eligible-pre-prune display mode
+was requested. `post_proof_retained_front=true` makes that semantic change
+explicit. Segment boundaries remain in the existing segment arrays. Captured
+isochrones remain historical retained populations; they are not current search.
+
+`route_result_v2` has a compatible optional addition:
+`diagnostics.coastalPruning` (`schemaVersion:1`), including `seedActions`.
+Existing Off JSON is unchanged. Missing incumbent/speed are JSON `null`, not
+zero. Consumers should capability-negotiate the C++/bridge feature separately
+from JSON/saved-plan versions.
+
+## Evidence and limits
+
+`test_coastal_pruning.cpp` covers analytic cap bounds against independent legal
+spherical paths, arrival regions, holes, narrow channels, incomplete domains,
+antimeridian symmetry, linear-polygon erosion, signed-distance uncertainty and
+identity, seed replay/failure/budgets, horizon partials, opening exclusions,
+unknown favorable currents, cancellation, audit, and deterministic workers.
+The duplicate-hole regression uses the review counterexample (400 repeated SW
+hole vertices), repeated outer/closing vertices, and near-duplicates. All variants
+preserve the water hole and the legal 1080.73 nm meridian bound; the pre-fix
+implementation incorrectly returned 1204.08 nm.
+The four existing strategic exhaustive-reference scenarios now also exercise
+Conservative with validated winning actions and bound checks: both mirrored
+delayed-wind and losing-first-tack/island cases retain the superior detour.
+The island tests explicitly verify that the default destination-bearing seed
+fails, while the supplied bounded `seed_actions` detour validates successfully.
+
+Controlled fixed-worker strait fixture, Release on AppleClang 21:
+
+| Work | Off | Conservative |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| Main expanded nodes | 37,334 | 1,180 |
+| Northern frontier parents | 6,081 | 9 |
+| Northern eligible-segment evaluations | 277,382 | 406 |
+| Seed integration work | 0 | 25 |
+| Native certified-cap admission units | 0 | 1 |
+| Paired measured wall time | about 1,980 ms | about 69 ms |
+
+The fixture asserts no ETA regression beyond two seconds, over 50% reduction
+in northern parents, fewer total main expansions, successful action replay,
+and deterministic two-worker results. Timings include seed/preparation and the
+requested progress payloads, but are not a universal performance promise.
+This version of the strait fixture supplies an analytically certified cap.
+Application-side NTS/rectangle certification is not timed here and must be
+reported separately in an application end-to-end benchmark.
+The separate `/usr/bin/time -l` paired-fixture process measured roughly 540 MB
+maximum RSS, including both runs, GRIB loading, replay, and a two-worker repeat;
+this is **not** a per-mode memory comparison.
+
+Validation:
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DSAILROUTE_ENABLE_ENSEMBLE=ON
+cmake --build build -j 4
+cd build
+./sailroute_tests coastal strategic cruising JSON progress isochrone environment landmask beam
+./sailroute_tests exclusion
+```
+
+93 selected routing tests and the eight-test exclusion group passed. Native/GSHHG real-world assets and a saved Juan de
+Fuca request were not supplied; synthetic fixtures are not reproduction of the
+screenshot. Certified disconnection, land-guided automatic seed construction,
+current-provider speed certificates, tight sparse-linear-coast bounds,
+destination caches, and per-mode RSS benchmarking remain unsupported/deferred.
+Incomplete or weak proofs retain states; they never enable aggressive pruning.
